{"id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch09_VariationsOfEnergy#lem:dc-ch9-3-8","title":"lem:dc-ch9-3-8","path":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch09_VariationsOfEnergy.md","line":618,"body":"","kind":"lemma","status":"not_started","tex":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch09_VariationsOfEnergy.tex#lem:dc-ch9-3-8","uses":["[[references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch09_VariationsOfEnergy#rem:dc-ch9-2-9]]","[[references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/formalization-support#lem:dc-ch9-3-7-orientation-det]]","[[references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch09_VariationsOfEnergy#lem:dc-ch9-3-8]]","[[references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch09_VariationsOfEnergy#lem:dc-ch9-2-2-schwarz]]"],"inspired_by":[],"bindings":[],"is_index":false,"body_html":"","formal":[],"tex_content":{"path":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch09_VariationsOfEnergy.tex","label":"lem:dc-ch9-3-8","line":1424,"source":"\\begin{lemma}[invariant vector of an orthogonal map]\n  \\label{lem:dc-ch9-3-8}\n\n\n  \\dcref{ch9:3.8}\n  Let $A$ be an orthogonal linear transformation of $\\mathbf{R}^{n-1}$ and suppose\n  that $\\det A=(-1)^n$. Then $A$ fixes a non-zero vector of\n  $\\mathbf{R}^{n-1}$.\n\\end{lemma}\n\\begin{proof}\n  \\uses{rem:dc-ch9-2-9, lem:dc-ch9-3-7-orientation-det, lem:dc-ch9-3-8, lem:dc-ch9-2-2-schwarz}\n  \\sketch\n  Since $A$ is orthogonal, $A-I=A(I-A^{T})$. Taking determinants in dimension\n  $n-1$ gives\n  $$\n  \\det(A-I)=\\det(A)(-1)^{n-1}\\det(A-I)=-\\det(A-I).\n  $$\n  Hence $\\det(A-I)=0$, so $1$ is an eigenvalue of $A$.\n\\end{proof}","html":" <p></p><section class=\"theorem\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Lemma</div></section>[invariant vector of an orthogonal map] <span class=\"tex-label\" id=\"tex-label-6c656d3a64632d6368392d332d38\"></span> <p></p><span class=\"tex-source-references\" role=\"list\" style=\"display:block\"><span class=\"tex-dcref\" role=\"listitem\" style=\"display:block\">1. ch9:3.8</span></span>Let <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$A$\">$A$</span> be an orthogonal linear transformation of <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\mathbf{R}^{n-1}$\">$\\mathbf{R}^{n-1}$</span> and suppose that <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\det A=(-1)^n$\">$\\det A=(-1)^n$</span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$A$\">$A$</span> fixes a non-zero vector of <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\mathbf{R}^{n-1}$\">$\\mathbf{R}^{n-1}$</span>.  <section class=\"theorem proof\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Proof</div><p><a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FDoCarmo%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh09_VariationsOfEnergy%23rem%3Adc-ch9-2-9\">↗ rem:dc-ch9-2-9</a> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FDoCarmo%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2Fformalization-support%23lem%3Adc-ch9-3-7-orientation-det\">↗ lem:dc-ch9-3-7-orientation-det</a> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FDoCarmo%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh09_VariationsOfEnergy%23lem%3Adc-ch9-3-8\">↗ lem:dc-ch9-3-8</a> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FDoCarmo%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh09_VariationsOfEnergy%23lem%3Adc-ch9-2-2-schwarz\">↗ lem:dc-ch9-2-2-schwarz</a> \\sketch Since <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$A$\">$A$</span> is orthogonal, <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$A-I=A(I-A^{T})$\">$A-I=A(I-A^{T})$</span>. Taking determinants in dimension <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$n-1$\">$n-1$</span> gives </p><p><span class=\"math\" data-display=\"true\" data-tex=\"\\[  \\det (A-I)=\\det (A)(-1)^{n-1}\\det (A-I)=-\\det (A-I).  \\]\">\\[  \\det (A-I)=\\det (A)(-1)^{n-1}\\det (A-I)=-\\det (A-I).  \\]</span></p><p> Hence <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\det (A-I)=0$\">$\\det (A-I)=0$</span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$1$\">$1$</span> is an eigenvalue of <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$A$\">$A$</span>. </p><span class=\"qed\" aria-label=\"证毕\" style=\"display:block;text-align:right\">□</span></section>","warnings":["部分 TeX 排版细节未完整还原（已保留可读内容）: sketch"]},"previous":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch09_VariationsOfEnergy#thm:dc-ch9-3-7","next":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch09_VariationsOfEnergy#rem:dc-ch9-3-9","relations":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch09_VariationsOfEnergy#rem:dc-ch9-2-9","title":"rem:dc-ch9-2-9","label":"rem:dc-ch9-2-9"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/formalization-support#lem:dc-ch9-3-7-orientation-det","title":"lem:dc-ch9-3-7-orientation-det","label":"lem:dc-ch9-3-7-orientation-det"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch09_VariationsOfEnergy#lem:dc-ch9-3-8","title":"lem:dc-ch9-3-8","label":"lem:dc-ch9-3-8"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch09_VariationsOfEnergy#lem:dc-ch9-2-2-schwarz","title":"lem:dc-ch9-2-2-schwarz","label":"lem:dc-ch9-2-2-schwarz"}],"backlinks":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch09_VariationsOfEnergy#thm:dc-ch9-3-7","title":"thm:dc-ch9-3-7","label":"thm:dc-ch9-3-7"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch09_VariationsOfEnergy#lem:dc-ch9-3-8","title":"lem:dc-ch9-3-8","label":"lem:dc-ch9-3-8"}],"copy_context":{"version":1,"level":0,"node":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch09_VariationsOfEnergy#lem:dc-ch9-3-8","title":"lem:dc-ch9-3-8","tex":"\\begin{lemma}[invariant vector of an orthogonal map]\n  \\label{lem:dc-ch9-3-8}\n\n\n  \\dcref{ch9:3.8}\n  Let $A$ be an orthogonal linear transformation of $\\mathbf{R}^{n-1}$ and suppose\n  that $\\det A=(-1)^n$. Then $A$ fixes a non-zero vector of\n  $\\mathbf{R}^{n-1}$.\n\\end{lemma}\n\\begin{proof}\n  \\uses{rem:dc-ch9-2-9, lem:dc-ch9-3-7-orientation-det, lem:dc-ch9-3-8, lem:dc-ch9-2-2-schwarz}\n  \\sketch\n  Since $A$ is orthogonal, $A-I=A(I-A^{T})$. Taking determinants in dimension\n  $n-1$ gives\n  $$\n  \\det(A-I)=\\det(A)(-1)^{n-1}\\det(A-I)=-\\det(A-I).\n  $$\n  Hence $\\det(A-I)=0$, so $1$ is an eigenvalue of $A$.\n\\end{proof}","source":"tex/references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch09_VariationsOfEnergy.tex","description":"","dependencies":[{"node":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch09_VariationsOfEnergy#rem:dc-ch9-2-9","title":"rem:dc-ch9-2-9","description":""},{"node":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/formalization-support#lem:dc-ch9-3-7-orientation-det","title":"lem:dc-ch9-3-7-orientation-det","description":""},{"node":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch09_VariationsOfEnergy#lem:dc-ch9-2-2-schwarz","title":"lem:dc-ch9-2-2-schwarz","description":""}],"dependencies_omitted":0,"unresolved_dependencies":[],"unresolved_omitted":0}}